      "id": 1,
      "text": "Holly Springs was founded in 1836 by European-Americans on land once occupied by the Chickasaw.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://hollyspringsms.gov/history-of-holly-springs/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1837 the town was incorporated as Holly Springs and became the seat of newly created Marshall County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://hollyspringsms.gov/history-of-holly-springs/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1855 the Mississippi Central Railroad linked Holly Springs to Grand Junction, Tennessee, and later the Kansas City, Memphis and Birmingham line crossed there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://hollyspringsms.gov/history-of-holly-springs/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rust College, founded in 1866 by the Freedman's Aid Society, remains Holly Springs’ historic black college.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
